## Executive Summary
<!-- ID: executive_summary -->
Council MCP is the durable source authority for hooks soon to be distributed. The authoritative chain is package hook defaults/registry plus runtime hook templates in `council_mcp`, rendered by `council update` compiler phases into downstream `.claude/hooks/*`, `.codex/hooks/*`, `.claude/settings.json`, and `.codex/config.toml`. Generated hook copies already present under `scribe_mcp/.codex/hooks` or repo-local generated outputs are stale/non-authoritative for this planning pass.

The main Scribe-adjacent latency risk is not inside Scribe MCP timing payloads. It is hook-side work after or around tool calls: `PostToolUse` observes `mcp__scribe__set_project`, writes local binding/session/active-state files, may call Council Web HTTP bind/get/log endpoints, and falls back to file logging. `SessionStart` may perform two HTTP calls with different timeout behavior: one short 1.5s session-context read and one regular hook-client `session_start` call using the configured default hook timeout. `PreToolUse` can do a 2s PID lookup only for targeted urgent-message blocks when persona cannot be inferred from tool input.

Blueprint should treat hook timing/telemetry as its own package, separate from Scribe MCP tool timing, and require install gating that proves generated hook surfaces came from Council MCP source/templates and that stale generated copies were pruned or regenerated.


